      "content": "Benchmarking a local coding agent model on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 requires a granular dissection of the hardware-software interface, specifically focusing on how the 24GB VRAM constraint interacts with the computational demands of modern Large Language Models (LLMs) and the specific architectural optimizations required for coding tasks. The RTX 4090, with its 16384 CUDA cores and 384-bit memory interface, represents the apex of consumer-grade inference hardware, yet it remains a bottleneck for models exceeding 30 billion parameters when running at full precision. Therefore, the analysis must center on quantization strategies, speculative decoding mechanisms, and memory management techniques that allow for viable inference speeds without catastrophic degradation in code generation quality. The primary metric of interest is tokens per second (tok/s) during both prefill and decode phases, alongside latency measurements for tool-use cycles, which are critical for agent workflows.\n\nMTP (Multi-Token Prediction) speculative decoding is a transformative technique for local inference, particularly on hardware with limited VRAM like the 4090. Traditional speculative decoding relies on a smaller draft model to propose tokens, which the larger target model then verifies. However, MTP allows the target model itself to predict multiple future tokens in parallel during the forward pass, effectively turning the autoregressive nature of LLMs into a parallelized process. On an RTX 4090, running a 7B to 13B parameter model with MTP enabled can yield significant throughput gains. For instance, a 7B model quantized to 4-bit (Q4_K_M) using GGUF format can achieve baseline decode speeds of 80-100 tok/s. With MTP enabled, predicting 4-8 tokens per step, the effective throughput can increase by 30-50%, pushing speeds to 120-140 tok/s. This is crucial for coding agents that must generate long sequences of code or documentation. The tradeoff lies in the increased VRAM consumption during the prefill phase, as the model must compute multiple heads simultaneously. For a 13B model, MTP can push VRAM usage to the brink of the 24GB limit, requiring careful offloading of non-essential layers to system RAM, which introduces latency penalties. The quality tradeoff is minimal if the MTP depth is calibrated correctly; however, if the model is too uncertain, the acceptance rate of speculative tokens drops, negating the",
